qft-qed-lagrangian-f42f7650.conceptual_definition:
The Gauge Invariant QED Lagrangian, 
\mathcal{L} = \bar{\psi}(i\gamma^\mu D_\mu - m)\psi - \frac{1}{4}F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
, is the fundamental equation describing the Quantum Electrodynamics (QED) theory. It encapsulates the interactions between charged fermions (like electrons) and photons, the quanta of the electromagnetic field, while respecting the principles of gauge invariance. The Lagrangian density is the core of the theory, from which equations of motion can be derived via the Euler-Lagrange equations, and it forms the basis for perturbative calculations in QED.
